nning."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Nick LaLota seeks reelection in a Long Island district that leans Republican by about four points. He enters the fall with a decisive financial advantage, having raised $3.8 million and holding $3.3 million on hand, while Democrat Chris Gallant has raised roughly $650,000 with $172,000 available. Expert raters overwhelmingly place the race in the Likely R column, with two outlets calling it Solid R.\n\nGallant's campaign drew heavy attention in mid-September after reports of past bondage-gear modeling, which he addressed defiantly, but the episode dominated coverage of his candidacy. A December poll sponsored by a Democratic-leaning firm showed LaLota narrowly ahead, and prediction markets give LaLota roughly an 80 percent chance. The race is competitive enough to monitor but favors the incumbent.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1953, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.158, "margin": -7.0}]}